----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Introduction

Maps in Java are used to store **key-value pairs**.

Unlike Lists and Sets, a **Map stores data using keys and values**, where each key is unique.

👉 Common Map implementations:

✔ HashMap
✔ LinkedHashMap
✔ TreeMap

---

🎯 Why Use Maps?

Maps are useful when you need:

* Fast data lookup
* Key-value storage
* Unique keys
* Efficient searching

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🗂️ 1. HashMap

`HashMap` stores key-value pairs using a **hash table**.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Features

✔ Stores key-value pairs
✔ Unique keys
✔ Allows duplicate values
✔ Allows one null key
✔ Does not maintain insertion order
✔ Fast insertion and retrieval

---

📌 Syntax

```java
HashMap<Integer, String> map =
        new HashMap<>();
```

---

💻 Example

```java
import java.util.HashMap;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        HashMap<Integer, String> students =
                new HashMap<>();

        students.put(101, "Rahul");
        students.put(102, "Priya");
        students.put(103, "Amit");

        System.out.println(students);
    }
}
```

---

📌 Output

```text
{101=Rahul, 102=Priya, 103=Amit}
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔗 2. LinkedHashMap

`LinkedHashMap` extends `HashMap` and maintains insertion order.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Features

✔ Maintains insertion order
✔ Unique keys
✔ Allows one null key
✔ Faster iteration than HashMap

---

💻 Example

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        LinkedHashMap<Integer, String> map =
                new LinkedHashMap<>();

        map.put(1, "Java");
        map.put(2, "Python");
        map.put(3, "C++");

        System.out.println(map);
    }
}
```

---

📌 Output

```text
{1=Java, 2=Python, 3=C++}
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🌳 3. TreeMap

`TreeMap` stores key-value pairs in **sorted order of keys**.

It internally uses a **Red-Black Tree**.

---

📌 Features

✔ Keys are automatically sorted
✔ Unique keys
✔ Duplicate values allowed
✔ Does not allow null keys

---

💻 Example

```java
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        TreeMap<Integer, String> map =
                new TreeMap<>();

        map.put(3, "Java");
        map.put(1, "Python");
        map.put(2, "C++");

        System.out.println(map);
    }
}
```

---

📌 Output

```text
{1=Python, 2=C++, 3=Java}
```

